<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aten</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aten was the disc of the sun worshipped as a god in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, depicted as a sun whose rays end in human hands. Already honoured under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, it was raised by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> in the fourteenth century BCE above all other gods, whose cults, especially that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, were suppressed. After Akhenaten's death the traditional cults were restored and the Aten's temples were dismantled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aten.jpg" alt="Aten" width="320" height="139" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AtonX · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aten.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1353 to 1336 BCE, represented the most disruptive governance crisis in the empire's history: his elevation of the <b>Aten</b> above all other gods, suppression of the Amun cult, and relocation of the capital to Akhetaten produced religious upheaval at home and diplomatic drift abroad, with Levantine vassals repeatedly…</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1350 BCE the pharaoh Amenhotep IV broke with this tradition: renaming himself Akhenaten, he exalted the <b>Aten</b>, the visible disc of the sun, above all other gods, closed or neglected their temples, had the name of Amun hacked out of monuments and founded a new capital at…</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Hymn to the Aten is the longest of several hymn-poems addressed to the <b>Aten</b>, the sun-disk worshipped as the supreme god under the 18th Dynasty pharaoh Akhenaten.</blockquote><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a></li></ul></section>
